WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:14.800
Four classrooms. One has a grid and a die. One has sheets and a cup of counters. One has a pile of paper slips. One has a booklet. They're all running the same model.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:25.980
Take one row: the word "the", from a picture book called The magpie. On a grid, it's tallies. On a ledger, it's marks in coloured boxes.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:37.080
In a cup, it's counters. In a pile of cutouts, it's slips of paper. Or it's just four numbers.

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:46.020
And the numbers are chances. "Fence" has six of the nineteen, about a third. "Dog" has four, about a fifth.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:57.280
Watch one sentence use all three kits. Start after a full stop, with a cup: seventeen counters. Draw red: "it".

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:10.680
The row for "it" has ten marks, so give each mark a face of a ten-sided die. Roll a one: "sits". Then "on", and "the": only one way to go.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:29.820
The row for "the", as nineteen slips. Shuffle, pull one out: "dog". Put it back. Now the four "dog" slips: two say "runs", two have a full stop. Shuffle and draw: full stop.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:41.180
Cup, die or slips, we use the same counts to choose the next word. Ten die faces sometimes need rounding; counters and reusable slips can match the counts exactly.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:59.280
So pick the kit that suits your room. A grid for pairs with a die. A ledger and a cup for a group sharing the jobs. Slips when you'd rather cut than count. Two rooms on different kits have built the same kind of model, and they can compare stories.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:07.440
You can try another kit with the same book. Keep the counts and change how you draw from them, then compare what you write.
